1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,960
Okay, next section, pulmonary infections.

2
00:00:02,180 --> 00:00:04,500
The key discussion here is going to deal with pneumonia.

3
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:07,760
Pneumonia is an infection of the lung parenchyma.

4
00:00:08,180 --> 00:00:10,240
It occurs when the normal defenses are impaired.

5
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:12,960
Some of the normal defenses include our cough reflex.

6
00:00:13,220 --> 00:00:15,080
If that's impaired, there's an increased risk.

7
00:00:15,080 --> 00:00:19,480
Of course, the idea here would be that we're not able to remove organisms and particles

8
00:00:19,480 --> 00:00:20,840
that we would normally cough up.

9
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:24,880
Damage to the mucociliary escalator can increase the risk for pneumonia.

10
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:29,340
Recall that our lung conducting system that brings air into the lung

11
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:33,800
is actually lined by respiratory epithelium, and respiratory epithelium is ciliated.

12
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:39,140
Now, those cilia are important because they beat upward and allow us to push the mucus

13
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:43,600
that's present in the conducting portion of the lung up along the airway

14
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:49,040
and then back down the throat so that we swallow the mucus that is normally produced.

15
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:54,900
Now, that's important because it helps us to clear anything that might be trapped within that mucus.

16
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:58,960
And so damage to the mucociliary escalator would increase the risk for pneumonia.

17
00:00:59,340 --> 00:01:03,800
Now, some examples of how you might damage that escalator would be if the patient has a viral pneumonia.

18
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:09,800
That would damage the respiratory epithelial cells, and then because there is no mucociliary escalator,

19
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:15,800
there would be an increased risk of developing a superimposed bacterial pneumonia on top of that viral pneumonia.

20
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:19,260
And we'll talk about that in more detail as we move forward.

21
00:01:19,260 --> 00:01:21,260
Mucus plugging also increases the risk.

22
00:01:21,260 --> 00:01:28,260
For example, you know that any time you block a tube, in pathology, the general principle is that you get infection behind the block.

23
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:34,260
And so mucus plugging would result in blocking of an airway, which would then increase the risk of infection distal to that block.

24
00:01:34,260 --> 00:01:39,720
clinically, the patients who have pneumonia present with fever and chills.

25
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:46,720
That's because the organisms often leak out into the blood, which then results in fever and chills.

26
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:49,720
There can be cough with yellow-green or rusty sputum.

27
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:54,720
The yellow-green would represent pus, and the rusty sputum representing blood.

28
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:58,180
Tachypnea with pleuritic chest pain can also be seen.

29
00:01:58,180 --> 00:02:01,180
This is particularly important, the pleuritic chest pain.

30
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:06,520
The idea here is that when you breathe in, you are stretching the pleura, and when you stretch the pleura, you get pain.

31
00:02:06,520 --> 00:02:12,680
And so that's called pleuritic chest pain, chest pain that arises when a patient expands the lung and stretches the pleura.

32
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:18,180
Of course, the pleura is enervated, and what would sensitize these particular nerves in this region

33
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:24,180
would be the presence of bradykinin and prostaglandin E2 that's generated by the inflammatory response.

34
00:02:24,180 --> 00:02:31,140
It's, of course, high yield to know that bradykinin and prostaglandin E2 are your two key mediators of pain.

35
00:02:31,140 --> 00:02:34,980
And then the patients will have decreased breath sound with dullness to percussion.

36
00:02:34,980 --> 00:02:38,580
The idea here is that the lung is like a drum, it's full of air.

37
00:02:38,580 --> 00:02:46,220
If you replace the air with consolidation due to the production of exudate from the inflammatory response,

38
00:02:46,220 --> 00:02:53,340
that exudate will then decrease the breath sounds in that region, along with resulting in a dullness to percussion.

39
00:02:53,340 --> 00:03:00,300
And finally, the white count will be elevated because the patient is infected, so you would expect to see an elevated white blood cell count.

40
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:03,300
Diagnosis is often made by performing chest x-ray.

41
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:09,300
Sputum gram stain can help to identify a bacteria, along with culture, which can help to subtype that.

42
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:16,300
Blood cultures are also useful because organisms will often be in the blood, as I've mentioned just a moment ago.

43
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:20,260
Now, classically on chest x-ray, there are three patterns that can be seen.

44
00:03:20,260 --> 00:03:29,260
And the first pattern would be where the pneumonia produces consolidation that takes over an entire lobe, and so that is called a lobar pneumonia.

45
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:38,260
Another possibility would be that you have consolidation that runs along the small airways, so it would be distributed in a patchy manner along the small airways,

46
00:03:38,260 --> 00:03:50,220
and that's called a bronchopneumonia, and then the third possibility is where you don't have consolidation, but instead you have inflammation within the interstitium of the lung.

47
00:03:50,220 --> 00:03:56,220
And, of course, the interstitium is the connective tissue of the alveolar air sac, and that's called an interstitial pneumonia,

48
00:03:56,220 --> 00:04:07,180
and the key pattern seen in an x-ray would be an increase in the lung markings, as if you're seeing, as if the interstitium of all of these air sacs becomes slightly more visible,

49
00:04:07,180 --> 00:04:15,180
so you begin to see an increase in the markings of the lung, and I'll make all this very clear as we move forward on a couple sample x-ray images.

50
00:04:15,180 --> 00:04:23,180
Now, important to note that when you think about lobar pneumonia and bronchopneumonia, which I've drawn here on the left, these are usually going to be bacterial.

51
00:04:23,180 --> 00:04:32,840
And then when you think about interstitial pneumonia, this is also called atypical pneumonia, and the organisms are not usually bacterial,

52
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:37,840
but you think about perhaps virus, and you'll see this as we move forward as well.

53
00:04:37,840 --> 00:04:44,840
Lobar pneumonia is a consolidation of the entire lobe, as I've already illustrated. Again, it's usually bacterial, as I've already stated as well.

54
00:04:44,840 --> 00:04:51,840
Most important to note on this particular slide is that the two most common causes of lobar pneumonia are strep pneumoniae,

55
00:04:51,840 --> 00:05:01,800
which represents about 95% of lobar pneumonia, and then Klebsiella, which is a very high yield bug to be aware of for the purposes of examinations especially.

56
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:04,800
Now, here's what a lobar pneumonia looks like. I've already highlighted this principle.

57
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:11,800
You get knockout of a lobe with consolidation, hence the term lobar pneumonia.

58
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:17,800
Now, were you to look at this under a slide, what you would see is that the air sacs, these circles here that I'm drawing,

59
00:05:17,800 --> 00:05:22,800
they represent the air sacs, and you can see that the air sacs are full of neutrophils,

60
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:27,760
which are all these cells present in the air sacs, along with this pink frothy material,

61
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:30,760
which represents the exudate of the pneumonia.

62
00:05:30,760 --> 00:05:36,760
And so, of course, if this was present within all the air sacs of a lobe, you would have wipe out of a lobe,

63
00:05:36,760 --> 00:05:39,760
and hence the picture that we just saw, which was the lobar pneumonia.

64
00:05:39,760 --> 00:05:44,760
Now, again, I've told you that the two key causes, 95% is streptococcus pneumoniae,

65
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:50,720
and as is stated in your text, this is the most common cause of community-acquired pneumonia,

66
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:53,720
classically seen in middle-aged adults and the elderly.

67
00:05:53,720 --> 00:05:57,720
So streptococcus pneumoniae, 95% of lobar pneumoniae.

68
00:05:57,720 --> 00:06:02,720
Kleptococcus pneumoniae represents about 5% of lobar pneumoniae,

69
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:07,720
and what's the highest yield to understand about this is that it is an enteric flora that is aspirated.

70
00:06:07,720 --> 00:06:11,720
So it's classically going to be seen in patients at increased risk for aspiration.

71
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:17,680
For example, elderly in nursing homes, alcoholics, these people are at increased risk for aspiration,

72
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:21,680
and so those are the classic patients that get Kleptococcus pneumoniae.

73
00:06:21,680 --> 00:06:25,680
Now, important to note that this bug has a thick mucoid capsule,

74
00:06:25,680 --> 00:06:29,680
and so when these patients present, they often present with current jelly sputum,

75
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:34,680
because part of what they cough up is this thick mucoid capsule.

76
00:06:34,680 --> 00:06:37,680
Now, Kleptococcus pneumoniae often is complicated by an abscess,

77
00:06:37,680 --> 00:06:42,640
so it's not uncommon for patients with Klebsiella to also develop an abscess.

78
00:06:42,640 --> 00:06:46,640
So those are some of the high yields to be aware of, especially for board examinations.

79
00:06:46,640 --> 00:06:52,640
Now, when a patient gets a lobar pneumonia, there are four classic phases that can be seen on pathology.

80
00:06:52,640 --> 00:06:55,640
The first is congestion, and the idea here is when you get infection,

81
00:06:55,640 --> 00:06:59,640
you're going to congest the blood vessels by dilating those blood vessels,

82
00:06:59,640 --> 00:07:03,640
increasing the amount of blood in the vessels, and also resulting in edema.

83
00:07:03,640 --> 00:07:07,600
So the very first phase is congestion, where you have congested vessels and edema.

84
00:07:07,600 --> 00:07:09,600
The second phase is called red hepatization.

85
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:13,600
The idea here is that you develop an exudate within the lung.

86
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:18,600
The exudate contains neutrophils and some blood, and that fills the alveolar air sacs,

87
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:21,600
and it gives the normal spongy lung a solid consistency,

88
00:07:21,600 --> 00:07:29,600
and so hence the term hepatization, or liver-like change to the normal spongy lung.

89
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:36,560
And, of course, it's red here because there's a lot of red blood cells that are also coming out into the alveolar airspace.

90
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:42,560
Eventually, those red blood cells are broken down, and so it turns gray, and then we call that gray hepatization.

91
00:07:42,560 --> 00:07:48,560
And finally, you resolve the exudate, and then you begin healing, and that's called resolution.

92
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:51,560
Very high yield to note that after a patient gets pneumonia,

93
00:07:51,560 --> 00:07:56,560
you pretty much heal the lung by regenerating the tissue that was normally present.

94
00:07:56,560 --> 00:08:02,520
And remember that the type 2 pneumocyte, very high yield, the type 2 pneumocyte is the stem cell of the lung

95
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:06,520
that helps to regenerate the lining of the alveolar air sacs.

96
00:08:06,520 --> 00:08:09,520
Here's what a pneumonia would look like on gross exam.

97
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:15,520
This represents the consolidated lobe, and so you can see that it becomes solid instead of spongy,

98
00:08:15,520 --> 00:08:22,520
and so that's where the term hepatization comes from, where it becomes much more like the liver than it does the normal spongy lung.

99
00:08:22,520 --> 00:08:28,480
And in this particular case, this is all full of red blood cells, and so this is red hepatization.

100
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:34,480
Once the red cells are broken down, this would then turn gray, become gray hepatization, and finally it would resolve.

101
00:08:34,480 --> 00:08:39,480
Bronchopneumonia is when you get scattered patchy consolidation centered around bronchioles.

102
00:08:39,480 --> 00:08:44,480
It's often multifocal and bilateral, and again, as I've stated, it's caused by bacteria.

103
00:08:44,480 --> 00:08:54,440
Here's a classic picture showing you a bunch of splotches or areas of pneumonia, and again, that would represent a bronchopneumonia.

104
00:08:54,440 --> 00:09:02,440
Here's a gross picture of bronchopneumonia highlighting the patchiness of the process, and of course, this is running along the bronchioles.

105
00:09:02,440 --> 00:09:06,440
So scattered along the bronchioles are these patches of inflammation.

106
00:09:06,440 --> 00:09:13,400
Now the key causes of bronchopneumonia include staph aureus, very high yield to note a few things, and I've listed them here.

107
00:09:13,400 --> 00:09:17,400
The first is that it is the most common cause of secondary pneumonia.

108
00:09:17,400 --> 00:09:25,400
Recall that secondary pneumonia is when you have bacterial pneumonia superimposed on a pre-existing viral infection of the lung.

109
00:09:25,400 --> 00:09:30,400
And again, the idea here is that the virus knocks out the mucociliary escalator.

110
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:33,360
That increases the risk to develop a pneumonia.

111
00:09:33,360 --> 00:09:41,360
The patients subsequently get infected with staph aureus, and that again results in what we call a secondary pneumonia.

112
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:51,360
Staph aureus is often complicated by abscess or empyema, which refers to pus in the plural space, and that's important to note as well.

113
00:09:51,360 --> 00:09:54,360
H-flu is another important cause of bronchopneumonia.

114
00:09:54,360 --> 00:10:02,320
This is a common cause of secondary pneumonia, which we've already talked about, and pneumonia superimposed on COPD.

115
00:10:02,320 --> 00:10:11,320
So this can result in, in patients with COPD, they can get superimposed pneumonia, which then leads to exacerbation of the COPD.

116
00:10:11,320 --> 00:10:14,320
Pseudomonas aeruginosa can also cause a bronchopneumonia.

117
00:10:14,320 --> 00:10:19,320
The classic patient who develops this is the cystic fibrosis patient.

118
00:10:19,320 --> 00:10:22,320
Moraxella can create a bronchopneumonia.

119
00:10:22,320 --> 00:10:31,280
This would be seen as a community-acquired pneumonia and a pneumonia that could be superimposed on COPD, again leading to an exacerbation of COPD.

120
00:10:31,280 --> 00:10:36,280
And then finally, legionella is an important cause of community-acquired pneumonia.

121
00:10:36,280 --> 00:10:42,280
It can also result in pneumonia superimposed on COPD or pneumonia in immunocompromised states.

122
00:10:42,280 --> 00:10:48,240
Very high yield to note for legionella is that it often arises from a water source.

123
00:10:48,240 --> 00:10:55,240
And then also important to note is that it is an intracellular organism that is best visualized by silver stain.

124
00:10:55,240 --> 00:11:01,240
A third pattern of pneumonia is interstitial pneumonia, and this is when you get diffuse interstitial infiltrates within the lung.

125
00:11:01,240 --> 00:11:06,200
Of course, the interstitium, as I've already explained, is a connective tissue of the alveolar air sac.

126
00:11:06,200 --> 00:11:16,200
So if these are the alveolar air sacs, you begin to have inflammation within the wall of the alveolar air sacs without any major consolidation.

127
00:11:16,200 --> 00:11:23,200
Interstitial pneumonia is often also called atypical pneumonia, and the word atypical helps me to remember a few things about interstitial pneumonia.

128
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:27,160
First of all, the signs and symptoms are relatively atypical.

129
00:11:27,160 --> 00:11:37,160
Remember that in the classic patient with pneumonia, you would expect high fever, chills, pleuritic chest pain, dullness to percussion, an elevated white blood cell count, etc.

130
00:11:37,160 --> 00:11:48,160
However, in interstitial pneumonia, the symptoms are much more upper respiratory symptoms, so that the patients have minimal sputum, they have cough, and they usually have a low fever.

131
00:11:48,160 --> 00:11:51,160
Here's a classic image for an interstitial pneumonia.

132
00:11:51,160 --> 00:12:02,120
When you look at the lung, you can clearly see that the interstitium is much more prominent, and you can clearly see that there's an increase in lung markings and interstitial processes occurring.

133
00:12:02,120 --> 00:12:09,120
Here's a picture of interstitial pneumonia on biopsy, and what you would see is that the air sacs are predominantly empty.

134
00:12:09,120 --> 00:12:13,500
You don't see very many cells or much of an exudate within the air sacs.

135
00:12:13,500 --> 00:12:18,500
However, instead, you see a lot of inflammatory cells within the wall of the interstitium.

136
00:12:18,500 --> 00:12:23,460
And again, it's this interstitial process that results in the increased markings on x-ray.

137
00:12:23,460 --> 00:12:30,460
The other thing that the word atypical helps me to remember when I consider an interstitial pneumonia is that the bugs are usually atypical.

138
00:12:30,460 --> 00:12:33,420
They're not your classic pneumonia bugs.

139
00:12:33,420 --> 00:12:36,420
The most common cause of atypical pneumonia is mycoplasma pneumonia.

140
00:12:36,420 --> 00:12:42,420
It's important to note that it usually affects young adults, classically military recruits or college students living in a dorm.

141
00:12:42,420 --> 00:12:48,420
One of the high-yield complications to be aware of is that it can produce an autoimmune hemolytic anemia.

142
00:12:48,420 --> 00:12:56,380
That's going to be an IgM, and so that's called a cold agglutinin, so it's one type of autoimmune hemolytic anemia.

143
00:12:56,380 --> 00:13:02,380
And finally, important to note that it's not usually visible on gram stain because it lacks a cell wall.

144
00:13:02,380 --> 00:13:06,340
So these are all the high-yields concerning mycoplasma.

145
00:13:06,380 --> 00:13:10,340
Chlamydia pneumonia is the second most common cause of atypical pneumonia in young adults.

146
00:13:10,380 --> 00:13:18,340
So if you have a young adult with the signs and symptoms of an atypical pneumonia and it's not mycoplasma, you want to think about chlamydia pneumonia.

147
00:13:18,380 --> 00:13:23,340
RSV is the most common cause of atypical pneumonia in infants.

148
00:13:23,340 --> 00:13:30,300
CMV is the classic cause of atypical pneumonia in patients who want post-transplant immunosuppressive therapy.

149
00:13:30,340 --> 00:13:36,300
Influenza is a classic cause of atypical pneumonia in the elderly.

150
00:13:36,340 --> 00:13:40,300
It can also be seen in the immunocompromised and those with pre-existing lung disease.

151
00:13:40,340 --> 00:13:46,300
Very high-yield to know that patients who get influenza, they increase their risk for secondary pneumonia.

152
00:13:46,340 --> 00:13:52,300
This is very important to understand because the vast majority of patients who get influenza virus as a cause of pneumonia

153
00:13:52,300 --> 00:13:57,260
that don't die because of the flu virus, they don't die because of the influenza virus.

154
00:13:57,300 --> 00:14:04,260
Instead what happens is the influenza virus weakens the normal defenses and that allows for a secondary bacterial pneumonia

155
00:14:04,300 --> 00:14:06,260
which then kills the patient.

156
00:14:06,300 --> 00:14:16,260
And so it's important to note that Staph aureus is the most common cause of secondary pneumonia in patients who have influenza virus pneumonia.

157
00:14:16,300 --> 00:14:22,260
Coxiella is an important cause of atypical pneumonia in farmers and veterinarians.

158
00:14:22,300 --> 00:14:27,260
It's classically associated with a high fever so it's called Q fever.

159
00:14:27,300 --> 00:14:32,260
Remember that normally when you think about interstitial pneumonia the fever is usually low

160
00:14:32,300 --> 00:14:36,260
and so it's important to note that these patients would have a high fever and hence the term Q fever.

161
00:14:36,300 --> 00:14:42,260
Now what happens in these particular patients is they get exposed to Coxiello spores

162
00:14:42,260 --> 00:14:47,220
and that's because those spores are deposited on the cattle by ticks or are present in cattle placentas.

163
00:14:47,260 --> 00:14:53,220
And so if a cattle recently gave birth the placenta could potentially be a source of infection.

164
00:14:53,260 --> 00:14:58,220
Now remember that from microbiology that Coxiello is actually a rickettsial organism

165
00:14:58,260 --> 00:15:03,220
but it's different from most other rickettsiae because number one it causes pneumonia, that's unique.

166
00:15:03,220 --> 00:15:09,180
Number two it does not require an arthropod vector, it survives as a heat resistant spore.

167
00:15:09,220 --> 00:15:14,180
And number three it doesn't produce a skin rash whereas most rickettsial organisms would.

168
00:15:14,220 --> 00:15:21,180
Aspiration pneumonia occurs in patients who are at risk for aspiration, for example alcoholics or patients who are comatose.

169
00:15:21,220 --> 00:15:25,180
This is classically due to anaerobic bacteria from the oropharynx

170
00:15:25,180 --> 00:15:31,140
and the three high yield bugs to be aware of are Bacterioides, Fusobacterium and Peptococcus.

171
00:15:31,180 --> 00:15:34,140
These are commonly asked on examinations.

172
00:15:34,180 --> 00:15:39,140
Recall that when patients get aspiration pneumonia the classic location is the right lower lobe

173
00:15:39,180 --> 00:15:43,140
which then results in an abscess and the right lower lobe is the classic location

174
00:15:43,180 --> 00:15:49,140
because anatomically the right main stem bronchus branches at a less acute angle than the left.

175
00:15:49,180 --> 00:15:55,140
So if this is the main airway and then you're looking at the branch, this is the right and the left is the right

176
00:15:55,180 --> 00:15:57,140
lower lobe is for example like that.

177
00:15:57,180 --> 00:16:01,140
So it's much easier to go down the right than it is to go down the left

178
00:16:01,180 --> 00:16:07,140
which then results in a right lower lobe abscess.

179
00:16:07,180 --> 00:16:10,140
The last of the pulmonary infections that we're going to discuss is tuberculosis.

180
00:16:10,180 --> 00:16:16,140
This arises with inhalation of aerosolized mycobacterium tuberculosis.

181
00:16:16,180 --> 00:16:19,140
Remember that when a patient gets exposed to TB they get primary TB.

182
00:16:19,180 --> 00:16:24,140
Now in primary TB the patient gets focal caseating necrosis in the lung,

183
00:16:24,140 --> 00:16:28,100
usually the lower lobe and also within the hyalur lymph nodes.

184
00:16:28,140 --> 00:16:32,100
Of course these foci are going to undergo fibrosis and calcification

185
00:16:32,140 --> 00:16:35,100
and that results in formation of something called the gon complex

186
00:16:35,140 --> 00:16:39,100
which is basically a area of fibrosis and calcification

187
00:16:39,140 --> 00:16:43,100
indicating that the patient has been exposed to primary TB.

188
00:16:43,140 --> 00:16:46,100
Now recall that primary TB is usually asymptomatic

189
00:16:46,140 --> 00:16:51,100
so all the classic signs and symptoms of TB will not occur with this initial exposure

190
00:16:51,100 --> 00:16:54,060
but the PPD will become positive.

191
00:16:54,100 --> 00:16:59,060
Of course the only real complication of primary TB is the formation of a gon complex

192
00:16:59,100 --> 00:17:04,060
that is a fibrosed and calcified nodule that arises within the lung.

193
00:17:04,100 --> 00:17:08,060
It's classically subplural so notice out here this is the pleura

194
00:17:08,100 --> 00:17:10,060
and this represents the gon complex.

195
00:17:10,100 --> 00:17:15,060
Now patients usually develop a gon complex within the subplural region of the lung

196
00:17:15,100 --> 00:17:17,060
along with the hyalur lymph nodes.

197
00:17:17,060 --> 00:17:21,020
Over time this gon complex can become reactivated

198
00:17:21,060 --> 00:17:24,020
and that would then result in secondary TB.

199
00:17:24,060 --> 00:17:27,020
Now this commonly arises due to AIDS.

200
00:17:27,060 --> 00:17:29,020
It can also be seen with aging

201
00:17:29,060 --> 00:17:31,020
and of course you know that because of AIDS

202
00:17:31,060 --> 00:17:35,020
there's been an increase in the incidence of tuberculosis.

203
00:17:35,060 --> 00:17:37,020
Often it occurs at the apex of the lung

204
00:17:37,060 --> 00:17:41,020
and that's because the oxygen tension is highest at the apex of the lung.

205
00:17:41,020 --> 00:17:46,980
Now once a patient gets reactivation then they develop foci of caseous necrosis

206
00:17:46,980 --> 00:17:48,980
they can also develop miliary pulmonary TB

207
00:17:48,980 --> 00:17:51,980
where you've got tiny little regions of TB

208
00:17:51,980 --> 00:17:54,980
that are sort of scattered across the entire lung

209
00:17:54,980 --> 00:17:57,980
or they can develop a bronchopneumonia due to TB

210
00:17:57,980 --> 00:18:00,980
so they would get this classic bronchopneumonia pattern

211
00:18:00,980 --> 00:18:02,980
but of course this would be due to TB.

212
00:18:02,980 --> 00:18:06,980
Clinically these are the patients that present with fever and night sweats

213
00:18:06,980 --> 00:18:11,940
cough with hemoptysis, weight loss, eventually a biopsy would be performed

214
00:18:11,940 --> 00:18:13,940
and it would reveal caseating granulomas.

215
00:18:13,940 --> 00:18:16,940
And of course the differential diagnosis of caseating granulomas

216
00:18:16,940 --> 00:18:19,940
includes fungus and TB.

217
00:18:19,940 --> 00:18:21,940
And so an AFB stain would be performed

218
00:18:21,940 --> 00:18:25,940
and that would reveal the presence of red acid-fast bacilli.

219
00:18:25,940 --> 00:18:29,940
This is the classic picture of a caseating necrotic granuloma.

220
00:18:29,980 --> 00:18:32,940
You can see the necrosis within the center of this granuloma

221
00:18:32,940 --> 00:18:36,900
along with the epithelioid histiocytes out here at the edge.

222
00:18:36,900 --> 00:18:38,900
Of course you would perform an AFB stain

223
00:18:38,900 --> 00:18:40,900
to confirm which organism is involved

224
00:18:40,900 --> 00:18:45,900
and in this particular case you can clearly see these red slender organisms

225
00:18:45,900 --> 00:18:47,900
which represent mycobacterium.

226
00:18:47,900 --> 00:18:52,900
Now remember that TB can also spread to multiple distant sites within the body.

227
00:18:52,900 --> 00:18:54,900
It can basically spread to any tissue.

228
00:18:54,900 --> 00:18:57,900
However there are a few high yield tissues to be aware of.

229
00:18:57,900 --> 00:19:00,900
First of all it can go to the meninges giving a patient meningitis

230
00:19:00,900 --> 00:19:03,860
and important to remember that when it goes to the meninges

231
00:19:03,860 --> 00:19:06,860
it usually involves the meninges at the base of the brain.

232
00:19:06,860 --> 00:19:08,860
That's particularly high yield.

233
00:19:08,860 --> 00:19:11,860
So meningitis with granulomas at the base of the brain.

234
00:19:11,860 --> 00:19:14,860
It can also involve the cervical lymph nodes.

235
00:19:14,860 --> 00:19:17,860
The most common organ to be involved is the kidney

236
00:19:17,860 --> 00:19:21,860
and when it involves the kidney it often produces a sterile pyuria.

237
00:19:21,860 --> 00:19:25,860
And finally when it goes to the bone we call that pock disease

238
00:19:25,860 --> 00:19:30,820
in particular when it involves the lumbar vertebral bodies.

239
00:19:30,820 --> 00:19:33,820
And so these are some of the key sites of spread outside of the lung.

240
00:19:33,820 --> 00:19:35,820
And that concludes our discussion of pulmonary infection.

